  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 120 AM CDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\nToday and tonight...\n\nA sinking surface boundary sliding southeast paired with the right\nentrance region an upper jet streak/speed max will contribute to\nan early morning round of elevated convection this morning across\nportions of the Delta and central MS. With this activity,\ntemperatures/heat indices will be cooler today than we've seen\nrecently with maximum values near 105 mostly near and south of the\nI-20 corridor. A \"Limited\" (1/4) risk for heat stress remains\ndepicted in these areas.\n\nMostly to the south of this early activity, afternoon heating\ncould allow for a rejuvenation of storm activity and these \ndiurnally stronger storms warrant continuation of a \"Slight\" \n(2/5) risk for severe weather with the primary risk expected to \nbe a risk for damaging wind gusts. \n\nTuesday into the weekend...\n\nA preciously advertised risk for strong to severe storms on\nTuesday has been withdrawn on this forecast cycle. Recent trends \nsuggest early morning convective activity could result in a \nsomewhat more thermodynamically stable environment during the\nafternoon and evening tomorrow. \n\nThrough the week, deep layer upper ridging will initially\nretrograde toward the Southwest as upper troughing generally moves\nfurther northeast and weaker away from the area. This results in\nupper heights remaining generally unchanged but with a somewhat\nreduced gradient. This could mean somewhat of a reduction in the\nnear daily risk of mostly diurnally driven strong to severe\nstorms, but embedded disturbances within the generally northwest \nflow aloft will mean some day-to-day variation could still bring \nthe threat to some portions of the area each day. This will be \nmonitored through the next week and targeted, mostly lower-end \nsevere weather outlooks for some risk of damaging winds could be \nintroduced when warranted. With a reduction in storm coverage \nlikely and a general return to weak, southerly boundary layer flow\nas opposed to the more recent northerlies, heat and humidity will\nbe on the rebound through the week. Heat headlines and advisories\nwill likely be needed as the week progress. /SW/86/",
